<section><exercise id="_12964d60-9f81-4912-977d-ded91307039c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=-3\) and \(x^2=9\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(-3)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 9=\frac{9}{2}=4\frac{1}{2}=4.5\)</para><para>The correct answer is 4.5. \(\frac{1}{2}(-3)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 9=\frac{9}{2}=4\frac{1}{2}=4.5\)</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="e3e9bc8c-3d92-4254-833a-c959d25c753d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=-2\) and \(x^2=4\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(-2)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 4=\frac{4}{2}=2\)</para><para>The correct answer is 2. \(\frac{1}{2}(-2)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 4=\frac{4}{2}=2\)</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="aebd7c88-b1e9-429d-8c26-0be943403732" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=-1\) and \(x^2=1\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(-1)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 1=\frac{1}{2}=0.5\)</para><para>The correct answer is 0.5. \(\frac{1}{2}(-1)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 1=\frac{1}{2}=0.5\)</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_19f7e9d6-11eb-42e9-8bc4-663b58043a4b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=0\) and \(x^2=0\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(0)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 0=0\)</para><para>The correct answer is 0. \(\frac{1}{2}(0)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 0=0\)</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_34793db7-7916-4c17-8e7d-5c350ef56e33" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=1\) and \(x^2=1\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(1)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 1=\frac{1}{2}=0.5\)</para><para>The correct answer is 0.5. \(\frac{1}{2}(1)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 1=\frac{1}{2}=0.5\)</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="a9602fee-e393-45f9-b26b-51f46f3fdc41" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=2\) and \(x^2=4\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(2)2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 4=\frac{4}{2}=2\)</para><para>The correct answer is 2. \(\frac{1}{2}(2)2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 4=\frac{4}{2}=2\)</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="a99a5c26-6a0d-4e72-82ed-ec405c1f1e47" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para/><para> When \(x=3\) and \(x^2=9\), what is value of \(\frac{1}{2}x^2\)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(\frac{1}{2}(3)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 9=\frac{9}{2}=4\frac{1}{2}=4.5\)</para><para>The correct answer is 4.5. \(\frac{1}{2}(3)^2=\frac{1}{2} \cdot 9=\frac{9}{2}=4\frac{1}{2}=4.5\)</para></solution></exercise></section>
